WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.010
Thank you! I'm the program manager for
the STEM Ambassador Program which is a

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:24.540
research and public engagement program
directed by Dr. Nalini Nadkarni, funded

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.750
by the National Science Foundation and
based at the University of Utah. I'm

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.600
gonna give an overview of the training
that we've developed in our outcomes

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.590
from our first two years of the program.
So I'll start by just saying the public

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:40.020
generally has a high opinion of science
science as a profession and this there's

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.980
a view that scientists are making a
valuable contribution to society. So

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.030
scientists are in a good position to
address the challenge of expanding the

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.440
reach of public engagement activities;
however, they need training to identify

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.860
appropriate audiences and venue for
engagement and in going beyond solely

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.460
transmitting science not knowledge
content to instead facilitate a dialogue.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.989
So our research team created STEM app to
build relationships for open-minded

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:07.260
exchange between the public and
scientists. We provide engagement to

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.260
science or engagement training to
scientists we refer to as STEM

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.010
ambassadors. These are scientists who are seeking to engage with the public

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:18.150
outside of traditional venues. That is
outside of museums, schools, and science

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.450
centers and this enables them to connect
with people who might not engage with

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:26.399
science in traditional ways. So we
present the scientist with several

00:01:26.399 --> 00:01:30.570
ambassador goals to add several
ambassador objectives to achieve this

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.380
overarching STEM app goal of
facilitating open-minded exchange.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.920
These objectives include demonstrating
shared values with members of the public,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.100
revealing that scientists have
identities outside of science that are

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.189
respectful of the identities of others,
showing that the scientific community

00:01:44.189 --> 00:01:48.090
cares about the public's well-being,
demonstrating a desire to learn from and

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.179
with others, and increasing the
accessibility of scientists to the

00:01:51.179 --> 00:01:55.500
public and the public to scientists. So
we don't emphasize the objective of

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.759
improving science literacy. We found that
many scientists enter the program with a

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:03.479
desire to fulfill this objective already
so we don't discourage this but we

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:07.049
challenge the scientists to achieve one
of these other ambassador objectives in

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:12.120
addition to any knowledge-based
objectives. So self-identity values and

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.110
our connection to others influences how
we receive and contextualize information.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.650
So in a recent paper Julie Risien and Martin Storksdieck proposed that

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.860
scientists can integrate the many
components of their identity as a

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.510
hobbyist, researcher, parent, and so on to
develop an impact identity which will

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.630
guide them in
doing engagement activities that they

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:34.340
find fulfilling and rewarding. So STEM
app draws on a scientists impact

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.610
identity to establish an authentic
connection with what we call a focal

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.850
group or a group gathered around a
shared interest, experience,

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:45.170
characteristic, or circumstance that
resonates with the scientist. So for

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:49.190
example we worked with an urban planner
who's studying water use in landscaping

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:53.120
and one facet of her impact identity
was her passion to connect with people

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.410
who face physical barriers to
participating in science in science.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.320
She was also really interested in
engaging those who were involved in

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.010
landscaping because she's her research
is concerned with outdoor water use and

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:08.690
landscaping practices. So she identified
inmates in a horticultural career

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.800
training program at a county jail as a
focal group who might share an interest

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.030
in water use and water wise landscaping
practices but who faced physical

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.900
barriers to participating in science and
so she ultimately partnered with the

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.710
jail's horticulture program to offer a
water-wise landscaping class to the

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.500
inmates.
We found that scientists can draw on

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:31.310
their impact identity to access a huge
diversity of audiences and venues

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:35.600
including adult and youth correctional
facilities, senior centers, cafes, Housing

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.340
Authority developments, and rock climbing
walls. Because the ambassadors go to so

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.760
many different places we found that
there was a need to develop our training

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.480
in such a way that we were imparting
generalizable skills that can be applied

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.010
to create targeted programs for each
focal group. So rather than provide

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.640
communication training specific to each
group which logistically would have been

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.400
really difficult we trained the
scientists to conduct what we call an

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.780
immersion visit. So scientists visit the
the focal group venue, they talk with

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:06.800
group representatives, they take notes on
the format and layout of the venue, the

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.430
shared values or experiences of the
group, and how the group communicates so

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:15.050
this is really bringing in that kind of
code design element. This visit

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:18.680
really serves to explore the connection
the Ambassador identified when

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:22.190
developing his or her impact identity
and it also serves to inform the design

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:26.200
of their engagement event. So after the
the scientist completes

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.580
their immersion visit, they apply a
design thinking process which is a

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:34.550
process that's traditionally applied by
designers to take a user-centered

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.450
approach to developing innovative
solutions. So scientists use this design

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:44.360
thinking process to transform their
immersion notes into an engagement activity that's
targeted to the focal group they plan to

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.740
work with. They use the notes from their
immersion to develop a guiding statement

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:53.029
that summarizes their observations and
their objectives and then they then use

00:04:53.029 --> 00:04:55.999
this statement to brainstorm
communication strategies and engagement

00:04:55.999 --> 00:04:59.389
activities that are specific to the
focal group. We then provide science

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:03.199
communication training which is derived
from the portal to the public program to

00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:07.219
help the scientists facilitate their
engagement activity. The activities take

00:05:07.219 --> 00:05:11.029
many different forms and are specific to
the focal group. They often overlap with

00:05:11.029 --> 00:05:14.389
pre-existing gatherings in the group
venue which allows the scientists to

00:05:14.389 --> 00:05:17.930
engage with people who might not
frequent informal science learning venues

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:23.930
or make a deliberate effort to go to a
science themed event. So I'll give a

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:27.139
couple examples of what some of these
look like. We had a biologist in our

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:32.120
program who has several science themed
tattoos and she facilitated a tattoo

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.029
event at a local coffee shop that was
located right next door to a tattoo

00:05:35.029 --> 00:05:38.749
parlor. She invited a tattoo artist from
the neighboring tattoo parlor to come

00:05:38.749 --> 00:05:41.629
over and participate in the event and
codesign the event with her so they

00:05:41.629 --> 00:05:45.889
were there to share their designs and
she was there to share the science

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:50.270
tattoos that she and her colleagues in
the biology department have. And so in

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.509
doing so she was able to reveal that
scientists have an interest in identity

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:58.580
outside of science that is this shared
interest in tattoos. We worked with an

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:03.289
oncologist who studies the cancer genome. She engaged with electricians. She

00:06:03.289 --> 00:06:06.860
described the techniques that she uses
to understand biological circuits and

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.669
map the cancer genome and then she
invited the electrician's to share the

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:15.229
tools that they use to map complex
electrical systems. So this really gave

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:18.620
her a fresh new way of thinking about
her research and demonstrated that

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.580
scientists are willing to learn from
others. We also worked with an atmospheric

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.210
scientist who was studying air quality.
He attended a children's festival that

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:30.259
was hosted by a community center serving
a low-income area. He presented a

00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:34.069
hands-on activity and shared his work to
address Salt Lake's air quality problem

00:06:34.069 --> 00:06:38.300
and in doing so was you know
demonstrating that scientists are

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.770
working on relevant community issues and
care about the community's well-being.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:46.900
So since we began in 2016 we've trained 46
scientists who have engaged roughly

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.940
nineteen hundred people through over a
hundred different engagement events in

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:51.400
40 unique venues.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.160
The majority of our trainings were

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.700
through an in-person workshop series, so
4 workshops occurring over several

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:00.520
weeks and then we worked with the
scientists to actually facilitate their

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.900
engagement activity and we're now
exploring an online format with a cohort

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:08.379
of eight scientists who were scattered
around the country and institutions

00:07:08.379 --> 00:07:12.069
outside of the University of Utah. Our
participant response has generally been

00:07:12.069 --> 00:07:15.340
positive. Over 90% of the participants
felt the scientist did a good job

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:19.120
communicating and tried to make the
interaction positive and survey and

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:21.789
interview data from our first two
cohorts have revealed that the

00:07:21.789 --> 00:07:23.979
scientists are finding their
participation in the program to be

00:07:23.979 --> 00:07:28.060
valuable as well. We observed an increase
in self assess science communication

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:31.509
confidence and ambassadors also
indicated that their participation in

00:07:31.509 --> 00:07:35.669
the program has piqued their interest in
engaging with new groups in the future.

00:07:35.669 --> 00:07:39.550
So we're finding that the process of
developing one's impact identity has

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:44.020
helped the ambassadors to realize new
engagement venues and opportunities. Many

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:47.710
scientists, 71% of the scientists that
entered our program, identified

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:53.469
opportunities a lack of opportunities
and a lack of venues as a barrier to

00:07:53.469 --> 00:07:57.520
their participation in public engagement.
We saw this go down to 29 percent upon

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.289
completion of the training. So to
summarize we've had success in guiding

00:08:02.289 --> 00:08:05.229
scientists and developing their impact
identity and this has been a

00:08:05.229 --> 00:08:09.120
collaborative effort of Nalini Nadkarni
at the University of Utah, Shelly Goldman

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.900
at Stanford University, Dennis Schatz at the
Pacific Science Center, Sue Allen at the

00:08:12.909 --> 00:08:15.460
Maine Mathematics and Science Alliance,
and Becky Menlove at the Natural

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:19.360
History Museum of Utah. We are also
working with an external evaluator at

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.520
Inverness Research and we're now really
working to explore strategies to sustain

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:28.630
and scale up this program. So I look
forward to sharing ideas with all of you

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:31.770
throughout the meeting and learning how
others have approached this challenge.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:35.780
Thanks!

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:59.959
There we go. I'm curious have you had any
scientists where it was hard to find

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:05.540
their impact identity or where you had
to work a lot to figure out what that group would be?

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:09.920
Yes, so we started by
doing one-on-one interviews with the

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.209
scientists to come up with their impact
identity and initially we really didn't

00:09:14.209 --> 00:09:18.470
have a set framework for these
interviews and so for some scientists

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:22.250
they would come in and say well I'm
studying such an obscure thing, I don't

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:26.269
know who would be interested in this or
how I can connect and so what we started

00:09:26.269 --> 00:09:30.019
doing was really breaking down the key
elements of who the scientist is and

00:09:30.019 --> 00:09:34.399
what they do. So we we developed an
exercise where we asked them a series of

00:09:34.399 --> 00:09:37.550
interview questions and then have them
generate these sticky notes with key

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:41.180
words related to their responses to the
questions and so the sticky notes can

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:44.900
include the scientist's hobby, you know
maybe they're a skier, a mountain biker,

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:49.160
and then also their research for their
microbiologist or physicist and then we

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:53.660
look at all of these keywords together
and use those two to brainstorm

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:58.519
potential connections. And now that
we've developed that exercise and now

00:09:58.519 --> 00:10:01.519
that we have a few examples of what
previous scientists have done so we're

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:05.480
creating kind of a database on our
website of what different biologists

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:09.110
have done, what different physicists have
done, we found that that process is

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.020
becoming a little bit easier

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:17.240
Okay so your program sounds super awesome!

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:21.860
You mentioned scaling up. Is this the
kind of thing that you would want to be

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:25.580
available like for free for other
scientists in other parts of the country

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:30.620
or just at your university. Now so we're
really interested in reaching scientists

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:35.420
in other parts of the country. We've been
piloting an online format which is

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:40.250
consisting right now of a series of
webinars and trainings for scientists. We

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.000
are finding though that there's a really
valuable in-person component to the

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:48.100
program so we're playing around with
some ideas right now of how

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:52.509
might you know work with scientists
remotely but still provide them with

00:10:52.509 --> 00:10:57.339
that in-person support or mentorship. But
yeah we're looking to engage with

00:10:57.339 --> 00:11:01.780
scientists around the country.

